  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/5/59/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_52.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Giant-Man is shown catching a gang of thugs, apparently led by a guy named Nathan Garrett, who is a \"famous research scientist\" - they were trying to sell secrets to the Red Chinese.&nbsp; Bail is set for him at one hundred thousand dollars, but he knows the Reds will pay it rather than risk him talking.&nbsp; Sure enough, they do, and he catches a plane out to a small country in Europe - a \"remote Balkan kingdom, nestled in the Alps.\"\n\nThere he finds a \"deserted castle\" (<i>oh no, didn't I just fuss about this in the last issue</i>?).&nbsp; He's fascinated by the legends of flying horses, and he puts his mind to use over the next \"days turn to weeks, to months\" period (<i>whoa!</i>) before he finally crosses eagle and stallion DNA to succeed!&nbsp; \"Weeks later\" after even that, back home in the States, Jan arrives (late) in Hank's lab and says she encountered a \"knight in black armor, riding a <i>mighty, flying horse</i>!\"&nbsp; While she watched, the Knight robbed an armored car and flew off!\n\nHank doesn't believe her, thinking she's just made up an excuse for being late, until they turn on the news (to which Jan says \"it will be a <i>pleasure</i> to hear something besides the rattling of your test tubes\" - <i>heh</i>).&nbsp; Sure enough, the coverage of the robbery is on the news.&nbsp; (\"I'm ready to accept your <i>apology</i> now, my dear Mr. Know-It-All!\")\n\nHank consults with his ant watch guards, and they find the Black Knight going after a helicopter.&nbsp; Giant-Man catches a lift on an airplane and jumps down and catches hold of the helicopter (without a parachute, I might add - and since it was a double-bladed copter, how on earth does he avoid hitting the propellers?&nbsp; I'm sure he doesn't practice this sort of maneuver!)&nbsp; Before he jumps, he says to the guy at the open hatch that if he misses the copter, \"tell the Wasp that - aw forget it!&nbsp; She must <i>know</i> how I really feel about her!\"\n\nDangling from the bottom of the copter, he's finally in a position to challenge the Black Knight, and when the latter speaks, Giant-Man thinks he recognizes the voice.&nbsp; Wrapped up in a tangle of bolo balls, Hank appeals to his vanity to goad him into monologuing and realizes now this is Professor Garrett, who proceeds to brag about all the weapons he's concealed in his lance.&nbsp; Finally Giant-Man drops and uses his free hand to take a reducing capsule, which allows him to get free of the bolo cables.\n\nWasp flies over to grab the now-Ant-Man-sized Hank and help lower him onto the flying horse unnoticed.&nbsp; He returns to normal size then and pokes the lance out of Black Knight's hand.&nbsp; The horse can't support both their weights and begins to lower toward an amusement park below, where Hank jumps off, leaving the Wasp to pinch the Black Knight, who also falls off, landing inside a roller coaster car, which leads him straight toward the now giant-sized Giant-Man!\n\nBut the Wasp tries to control the horse by becoming normal-sized to no avail, and it throws her.&nbsp; Hank is forced to leave the Black Knight to rescue her.&nbsp; By the time they get back to him, the horse has returned to its master, and he flies off - \"You've beaten me <i>this</i> time, Giant-Man - but I'll be back!&nbsp; Sooner or later  -- <i>I'll be back</i>!\"&nbsp; Jan wonders why all Hank's enemies make just cornball exit speeches, and Hank actually calls her \"honeychile\" - presumably coined from the 1951 film of the same name.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "nothing really noteworthy here to warrant a higher grade.&nbsp; The story flowed okay, and the star characters were pretty much on-point with their established personalities.&nbsp; And quite frankly, for <i>Tales to Astonish</i>, this wasn't a bad issue, which is <i>saying</i> something, as this book has largely been dreadful.\n\nA \"deserted castle?\"&nbsp; Really?&nbsp; Okay, admittedly this is Europe, where there are infinitely more castles than near NYC, but <i>wow</i>!&nbsp; I <i>literally</i> just called this out as a trope in the previous review!&nbsp; I don't know, man - perhaps Stan Lee had a secret desire to occupy an abandoned castle or something!\n\nThe timeline on this issue is very stretched out.&nbsp; The time between Giant-Man's initial capture of the gang of thugs is \"months\" before Nathan Garrett becomes the Black Knight, and then more \"weeks\" before he debuts himself in NYC.&nbsp; Stan Lee is pretty liberal with the passage of time here.&nbsp; Hank only became Giant-Man fairly <i>recently</i>, so to have so many months elapsed within one issue is pretty \"wasteful.\"&nbsp; I'll keep an eye on this phenomenon as I proceed onward...\n\nHank throwing himself out of a place and <i>down</i> toward a helicopter with two spinning blades seemed <i>terribly</i> reckless, and Jan seems to be around mostly for the ride and provides almost nothing to the final outcome other than preventing Hank from falling to his death.&nbsp; I imagine she might have been at least <i>some</i> use buzzing around his head to distract the Black Knight.\n\nFor the record, this is not the same Black Knight as Dane Whitman, who comes later, but <i>this</i> Black Knight does make other appearances in the near future, which we'll get to soon enough!\n\nDick Ayers does the art for this story, and I have to admit it was pretty good.&nbsp; I know I've commented before that he should stick to inking, but I'm big enough to give credit where it's due - good work on this issue!&nbsp; And he even remembers to omit the wings on the Wasp when she's full-sized, something Kirby doesn't seem to keep track of!&nbsp; Kudos!&nbsp; (Note that the Announcements section of [LINK:1963:FF_23|FF #23|2027-03-16] points out that he did a bang-up job.)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nOn the whole, the \"deserted castle,\" expanded timeline, and needless reckless behavior by Hank soured it enough to drop it below the VG/FN grade it might otherwise have gotten.",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale</b>:&nbsp; \"Not What They Seem!\"\n\nIn the back-up tale, the Wasp once again shows up at an orphanage to amuse the kids with a story, this time about escaped convicts in the year 3000, who head out into space to find a place to hide, and of course they meet up with a Twilight Zone-like twist ending!&nbsp; This is <i>Tales to Astonish</i> after all!&nbsp; The word \"mannequin\" is again spelled \"manikin,\" which continues to look odd, though not actually incorrect."
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
